and so we come to the end of another  academic year at Yale classes are almost  over exams are almost over on tuesday we  face that strange time of transition  where the tasks of daily life change for  many of us where that routine that set  up a set our life in order changes for  students the end of the semester often  means returning to that ambiguous place  called home that is still home but not  quite home anymore for others of us are  often our summer journeys mean visiting  with those with whom we were once close  family or friends people we once spent  great deal of time with but no longer in  contrast to the immediacy of daily life  at Yale these travels ask longer-term  questions of identity and belonging I  don't know about you but as I meet old  friends after having been apart for a  year or many years I find there is that  often awkward period when we tentatively  asked ourselves in our heads is this  still the same person that I was friends  with does he or she still care about me  or do they just care about the person  that I was then and their memories do I  have anything in common with this person  anymore even people in our own families  after some gentle probing there's  usually one moment in the interaction  where we find a shared memory that  speaks to both of us some experience  that we shared or a time in our lives  that brings us back together a truth  about one another that still applies or  best of all laughter about how clueless  we once were I think that draws me  together best with old friends and  suddenly the months or the years past  fall away and we can reclaim connection  with one another I think this is a taste  of what st. John meant when he had Jesus  say abide in me as i abide in you  rediscovering a connection what I want  to know from my old friend is that some  part of me still abides in them after  all these years not the me of two or  twenty years ago not some ideal picture  of who I was but me or some part of me  the essence of me abiding in their heart  waiting to be rediscovered st. john's  gospel is the mystical gospel of the for  full of meditations on jesus as bread or  water or light or life and in today's  Gospel the image of the vine and the  branches is poetic it's not literal it  wants in a hundred ways to say over and  over again we are like each of us the  tendrils of a vine each reaching out in  our own direction but ultimately rooted  in the vine and the soil itself in God  in His perhaps obscure or poetic way  john wants us to remember we are  connected to Christ and to the God he  calls father and so in this season of  goodbyes and greetings at our church  seems like connection is a good issue  for us to think about as we separate how  are we still connected as new people  come how do they become part of our  community for those leaving it's  questions like will I ever find another  Church like this one or do I even want  to will people here at Yale still  remember me after I'm gone and for those  staying what will happen after all these  people i know have left and we get a new  associate pastor and new people will I  still recognize this place as human  beings no matter how fiercely  independent or ironically distant or  cool we are I think we all yearn in some  way to be known to be accepted to be  connected in a huge chunk of the middle  of st. John's Gospel that we're reading  from today Jesus is trying to prepare  his friends and his disciples for his  coming debt death and resurrection in  these recurring and repeating metaphors  Jesus tries to say  though you will not see me we will still  be connected don't worry about in me as  i abide in you Jesus's ministry his  whole ministry has been devoted to  assuring those that have been told by  the world that they are not worthy of  God or that they are rejected by God  even that they are accepted and loved  over and over again he says it Jesus  finds a hundred ways to say yes you you  you are the one God loves you are the  lost coin you are the lost sheep you are  the prodigal child and Jesus goes on a  step further and John and says not only  are you loved by God you are connected  directly to God God abides in you as you  abide in God you are interwoven with God  like the English ivy on the walls of  battelle chapel but st. John's Jesus  would not have spent so many chapters  imagining one image after another about  connection if that connection were easy  John says it so often because it's hard  for so many reasons we can feel  disconnected from God first perhaps  because the world we live in god bless  it is so secular and scientific and  materialistic and so denuded of spirit  that it completely misunderstands God in  the wonderful new play that's playing at  their rep right now called the realistic  joneses there's a wonderful scene where  Parker Posey who plays a young woman in  there is trying to pray that she knows  nothing about praying she knows  something's wrong with her husband and  she wants to pray but she doesn't know  quite what to do so sitting alone on the  stage she says to god this feels weird  no offense you probably just think I'm  one of so many people you're probably  like my god what does this even about  maybe you're going to burn everything  down anyway I don't know you're crazy  mind you're going to burn everything  down  many of those around us have the  weirdest ideas about who or what God is  and your heart aches for them at those  critical moments when they need to abide  in something but have nothing to abide  him but we ourselves live lives that do  little or nothing each day to remind us  that we are connected to each other or  to God it's hard to do world doesn't  make it easy but we also struggle with  connection with God because we hunger  for personal independence and identity  we want to be ourselves and we want to  do it by ourselves Nadia bolz-weber in  her blog writes that the vines from st.  John are all tangled and messy and how 